      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{sin}^{2}⁡(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=9{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=18u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =18u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
